import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.8Wkhwg0B.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Basis sets","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"basis.md","filePath":"basis.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"basis.md"};function d(n,e,r,l,c,h){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="Basis-sets" tabindex="-1">Basis sets <a class="header-anchor" href="#Basis-sets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Basis sets {#Basis-sets}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>QuantumEngine.Basis</code> reads Gaussian basis sets in the Gaussian94 <code>.gbs</code> format and builds the molecular basis of a molecule with <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.build_basis"><code>build_basis</code></a>. The package ships 72 basis files from the Basis Set Exchange in <code>data/basis_sets/</code>; their source, license and citation are in that directory&#39;s <code>README.md</code>.</p><h2 id="Cartesian-functions-only" tabindex="-1">Cartesian functions only <a class="header-anchor" href="#Cartesian-functions-only" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cartesian functions only {#Cartesian-functions-only}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every shell is expanded in Cartesian Gaussians <code>x^lx y^ly z^lz exp(-α r²)</code>; no spherical (pure) functions are formed. A d shell therefore carries 6 functions, an f shell 10 and a g shell 15 (<code>ncart(l) = (l + 1)(l + 2)/2</code>), where a code that uses spherical functions has 5, 7 and 9. The number of basis functions, and so the energies, differ from those of a code that uses 5d (or 7f) functions with the same basis name; compare only with a calculation that also uses Cartesian functions.</p><p>Each Cartesian component has its own normalization, <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.primitive_norm"><code>primitive_norm</code></a><code>(α, lx, ly, lz)</code>, so <code>xy</code> and <code>xx</code> differ. The contraction of a shell is renormalized with <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.contraction_renorm"><code>contraction_renorm</code></a>, and the stored coefficients of a <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.Shell"><code>Shell</code></a> include that factor but not the per-component norm, which the consumer applies.</p><h2 id="Component-order" tabindex="-1">Component order <a class="header-anchor" href="#Component-order" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Component order {#Component-order}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.cartesian_component"><code>cartesian_component</code></a><code>(l, i)</code> gives the exponents <code>(lx, ly, lz)</code> of the <code>i</code>-th function of a shell, in the oracle&#39;s order, which is the order of the Molden format for <code>l ≤ 4</code>. No rule generates it (l = 3 lists <code>xyy</code> before <code>xxy</code>), so it is a table:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">l</th><th style="text-align:right;">shell</th><th style="text-align:right;">components, in order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">0</td><td style="text-align:right;">s</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>1</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">1</td><td style="text-align:right;">p</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>x y z</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">2</td><td style="text-align:right;">d</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>xx yy zz xy xz yz</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">3</td><td style="text-align:right;">f</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>xxx yyy zzz xyy xxy xxz xzz yzz yyz xyz</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">4</td><td style="text-align:right;">g</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>xxxx yyyy zzzz xxxy xxxz xyyy yyyz xzzz yzzz xxyy xxzz yyzz xxyz xyyz xyzz</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">5</td><td style="text-align:right;">h</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>xxxxx yyyyy zzzzz xxxxy xxxxz xyyyy yyyyz xzzzz yzzzz xxxyy xxxzz xxyyy yyyzz xxzzz yyzzz xxxyz xyyyz xyzzz xxyyz xxyzz xyyzz</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="The-l-limit" tabindex="-1">The l limit <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-l-limit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The l limit {#The-l-limit}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The component table stops at l = 5 (h). <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.parse_gbs"><code>parse_gbs</code></a> reads I shells (l = 6), which seven shipped files hold for some elements, so every shipped file parses. <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.build_basis"><code>build_basis</code></a> throws only when it places an I shell on an atom:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>basis: &lt;file&gt;:&lt;line&gt;: I shell (l = 6) for C; this build supports l ≤ 5</span></span></code></pre></div><p>So water in <code>def2-universal-jkfit</code> builds (its I shells belong to transition metals and heavier elements), while carbon in <code>cc-pv6z</code> throws.</p><h2 id="Basis-names" tabindex="-1">Basis names <a class="header-anchor" href="#Basis-names" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Basis names {#Basis-names}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A basis name is normalized by <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.normalize_basis_name"><code>normalize_basis_name</code></a>: stripped, lowercased, and mapped through the alias table, so <code>6-31G*</code>, <code>631g(d)</code> and <code>6-31g(d)</code> all name <code>6-31g_star</code>. A name not in the table is kept as it is. <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.candidate_stems"><code>candidate_stems</code></a> lists the file stems tried, in order: the normalized name first, then variants with <code>*</code>, <code>**</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>++</code>, <code>(d)</code> and <code>(d,p)</code> written out (<code>_star</code>, <code>_star_star</code>, <code>_dp</code>, <code>plus</code>, <code>plusplus</code>). A name containing <code>/</code> or <code>\\</code> is rejected on every platform; put the file in a directory on the search path instead.</p><p>Four aliases differ from the oracle&#39;s table:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">names</th><th style="text-align:right;">file stem here</th><th style="text-align:right;">the oracle&#39;s file</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-31G**</code>, <code>6-31g(d,p)</code> and their spellings</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-31g_star_star</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-31g_dp</code>, whose O d exponent differs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-311++G**</code>, <code>6-311++g(d,p)</code> and their spellings</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-311plusplusg_star_star</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-311plusplusg_dp</code>, with one shell fewer for C, N, O, F and Ne</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-311++G(2d,2p)</code> and its spellings</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-311plusplusg(2d,2p)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>6-311++g_2d2p</code>, which holds only H, C and N, identical to their blocks here</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>def2-SVP-JKFIT</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>def2-universal-jkfit</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the oracle&#39;s own placeholder file (H, C, N and O only)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The oracle&#39;s three files have no Basis Set Exchange header and are not shipped; the aliases name the Basis Set Exchange files of the same basis. <code>def2-SVP-JKFIT</code> is the oracle&#39;s default auxiliary basis; here it is read as the universal JK fit, and <code>build_basis</code> with a <code>logger</code> writes the note <code>basis &lt;name&gt;: the oracle&#39;s def2-SVP-JKFIT is a placeholder; using def2-universal-jkfit</code>.</p><h2 id="Search-order-and-shadowing" tabindex="-1">Search order and shadowing <a class="header-anchor" href="#Search-order-and-shadowing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Search order and shadowing {#Search-order-and-shadowing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.locate_basis_file"><code>locate_basis_file</code></a> tries, for each candidate stem in turn, each directory of <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.basis_search_paths"><code>basis_search_paths</code></a> in turn, and returns the absolute path of the first <code>&lt;dir&gt;/&lt;stem&gt;.gbs</code> that exists. The directories are, in order:</p><ol><li><p><code>basis_sets</code>, <code>../basis_sets</code> and <code>../../basis_sets</code>, relative to the working directory at the time of the lookup;</p></li><li><p><code>QUANTUM_ENGINE_BASIS_DIR</code>, when it is set and not empty;</p></li><li><p>when an executable directory is given, <code>basis_sets</code>, <code>../basis_sets</code> and <code>../../basis_sets</code> under it;</p></li><li><p>the package&#39;s <code>data/basis_sets</code>.</p></li></ol><p>The first match wins, so a file in a <code>basis_sets</code> directory under the working directory, or in <code>QUANTUM_ENGINE_BASIS_DIR</code>, shadows the shipped file of the same stem. Because the stems are the outer loop, an earlier stem found in a later directory wins over a later stem found in an earlier one. A name with no file throws a <code>BasisError</code> that lists the stems and the directories tried; there are no other fallbacks.</p><h2 id="Building-the-molecular-basis" tabindex="-1">Building the molecular basis <a class="header-anchor" href="#Building-the-molecular-basis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Building the molecular basis {#Building-the-molecular-basis}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.build_basis"><code>build_basis</code></a><code>(mol, name)</code> reads the whole file once with <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.parse_gbs"><code>parse_gbs</code></a> and gives each atom, in order, the shells of its element in file order. A ghost atom gets the shells of the element it stands for. The AO offsets run from 0 in shell order; <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.ao_range"><code>ao_range</code></a> gives the AOs of a shell and <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.shell_pairs"><code>shell_pairs</code></a> iterates the shell pairs. <code>build_basis(mol, name, Float32)</code> builds the basis in another element type.</p><p>ECP sections of a <code>.gbs</code> file are read into <a href="/PureAtomistic.jl/QuantumEngine/dev/api#QuantumEngine.Basis.ECPPotential"><code>ECPPotential</code></a>; ECP integrals are not implemented.</p><p>The functions and types are listed in the <a href="./api">API</a>, under &quot;Basis sets&quot;.</p>',25)])])}const p=t(o,[["render",d]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
